body:
Even worse: if you write `1.2`, Godot modifies the value to `1.19999999999999996`. This may be the same _float_, but it's not the same _number_. (This is why bank applications don't use floats for currency amounts.)

It may be true that Godot's `JSON` class parses numbers as floats, and so it ultimately doesn't matter if you only read JSON files using the `JSON` class. But this is a rather strong assumption to make:
- one could write their own JSON parser (it's a rather trivial format to write a parser for, the only slight difficulty is handling `\u`);
- there is almost always another program that will read your JSON files: Git;
- external editors (e.g. dialogue editors were mentioned earlier) can read and write JSON files;
- there are probably better editors for JSON files than Godot's editor out there.

In my case, I happen to be generating some JSON files, and writing some others using Emacs. Godot's reformatting messes with version control in both cases.  In addition, when I manually write `1.2`, I'd rather keep `1.2` in the file, even if technically the actual value is ultimately 1.19999999999999996.

@dalexeev I appreciate the rundown of options, but here's what I really don't understand.

Why is JSON fundamentally different than a text file? If I create a `example.txt` file in a Godot project directory, it is stored, byte-for-byte, as the text I put in it. Godot doesn't replace spaces with tabs. It doesn't add decimals to zeroes. It would be _ludicrous_ to suggest that it should do so. But when someone asks the same question of a file with `.json` as the extension, it seems to become a hard problem. I don't understand why we can't just... have the editor read the bytes of the JSON file and save back bytes of a JSON file, instead of treating it like there's a platonic ideal in the middle that loses formatting information.

At runtime, sure, whatever, parse it and store it in whatever internal data format you like in RAM. I don't care what happens at runtime. I'm not version controlling my runtime, and I don't care if the file diffs look ugly _at runtime_. I'm talking about behavior _in the editor_.

I ended up renaming all my JSON files to use a custom extension, and adding a load function like this:

```csharp
public static FileAccess LoadFile(string path, FileAccess.ModeFlags flags, out string newPath)
    {
        newPath = path.Replace(".json", ".myjson"); // output the actual path for logging, etc.
        return FileAccess.Open(newPath, flags);
    }
```
It's really hacky, but it's the only workaround I found that actually works and it's easily reversible if/when this issue gets resolved.
